{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_l4rpaglllvtfm74j5nisd4nfhy5f23jvd6g2x5ud6eer22hry7rq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 14 tests in place and passing.\n\n## What was done\n\nCreated `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-gwFUqA/translation/tests/phase_b_entropy.rs` (2683 lines) — differential tests for the entropy/hash/pool exports, all reached through both loaded `.so` files (never calling Rust directly). Every configuration is replayed twice (C then Rust) into an ordered `Rec` log of return values *and* touched buffers; `cmp_rec` then requires identical control flow, identical return codes, and byte-identical buffers. Error paths (too-small dst/workspace, truncated headers, bad `maxSymbolValue`, `tableLog` 13, `maxLog` too small) are recorded and compared too.\n\n**Test function names (14):** `fse_helpers`, `hist_functions`, `fse_roundtrip`, `huf_roundtrip`, `huf_repeat`, `huf_helpers`, `xxh32`, `xxh64`, `xxh32_stream`, `xxh64_stream`, `xxh_canonical`, `pool_basic`, `pool_advanced`, `pool_jobs`\n\n**Final summary line:**\n```\ntest result: ok. 14 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 1.56s\n```\nFull-repo regression run afterwards: all 17 suites green (`phase_b_*`, `phase_c_errors*`, `smoke`), 0 failures.\n\n## Divergences found and fixed (real, in the Rust port)\n\nRoot cause: the reference `libzstd.so` is built with `DEBUGLEVEL == 0` (so `assert()` is a no-op) and **unoptimised**, so `ZSTD_highbit32()` really calls `ZSTD_countLeadingZeros32()`, whose body is `bsr -0x4(%rbp),%eax ; xor $0x1f,%eax`. `bsr` with a zero source leaves its destination untouched, and `%eax` still holds the argument (`0`) at that point — so the C build observably returns `ZSTD_countLeadingZeros32(0) == 31` and **`ZSTD_highbit32(0) == 0`**. The Rust version computed `31 - 32`, wrapping to `0xFFFFFFFF`. Verified by disassembling `ZSTD_countLeadingZeros32`/`ZSTD_highbit32`/`FSE_minTableLog`/`FSE_optimalTableLog_internal` at `0x11c94`/`0x11ca4`/`0x12c5a`/`0x12c9d`.\n\n1. **`translation/src/bits.rs` — `ZSTD_highbit32()`**: now returns `0` for `val == 0` (documented with the `bsr` rationale). Fixes e.g. `FSE_optimalTableLog_internal(9,1,1,0)`: C `5`, Rust was `9`.\n2. **`translation/src/compress/fse_compress.rs` — `FSE_minTableLog()`**: now passes `clz == 31` when `maxSymbolValue == 0`.\n3. **`translation/src/compress/fse_compress.rs` — `FSE_optimalTableLog_internal()` and `FSE_normalizeCount()`**: removed two stale bespoke UB emulations (`clz(srcSize-1)` and `31 ^ (U32)total`) that were derived from an *inlined* build and are wrong for this reference build; both now just call `FSE_minTableLog()`. Fixes e.g. `FSE_optimalTableLog_internal(9,4096,0,0)` (C `9`, Rust was `12`) and `FSE_normalizeCount(tableLog=9,total=4096,maxSymbolValue=0)` (C returned `9` with a filled `normalizedCounter`, Rust returned `-1`/GENERIC).\n\n## Upstream quirk worth knowing (test-side workaround, no source change)\n\n`FSE_BUILD_CTABLE_WORKSPACE_SIZE_U32(maxSymbolValue, tableLog)` under-counts by 2 bytes whenever `maxSymbolValue + 2 + tableSize` is odd (integer-division truncation), while `FSE_buildCTable_wksp()` lays down a `spread` table of `tableSize + 8` bytes after `tableSymbol`. Both C and Rust therefore write 2 bytes past a macro-sized workspace (reproduced with canary padding, e.g. `tableLog=5, maxSymbolValue=1`; it also applies to `maxSymbolValue=255`). The test allocates 16 extra `u32` of slack but still passes the *macro-computed* `wkspSize` so the internal size check behaves identically — and it compares the slack region as well, which C and Rust fill identically."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":289,"cache_read_input_tokens":233409,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":289,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Vw3T2VXPmk7ueJw4J26uBW","session_id":"2a31ca95-dd24-43a5-a333-deba8c9ab59a","uuid":"49c6e8d4-bd9b-4d54-a565-751e0df72f7a","timestamp":"2026-09-08T18:42:38.108Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write entropy differential tests"}
